"""VIOLATION FIXTURE - the paste that happens once, at six in the evening, to check a thing works.

Nobody adds a key to a source file on purpose. They add it to see whether the call works at all,
intend to move it to the environment, and then the branch gets pushed. That is the entire threat
model, and it is why the guard exists before any key does.

## Why this is a `.template` and not a `.py`

The first version of this fixture was a real `.py` file carrying a credential-shaped literal, and
`make ci` went red on the commit that added it - the secret guard caught its own fixture, because a
fixture is a tracked file and "a key pasted into a test fixture" is one of the threats listed in the
guard's own docstring.

The obvious fix is an allowlist exempting this path. That is worse: an allowlist is a hole, it needs
maintaining, and the one thing it would exempt is the directory a careless paste is most likely to
land in. So the tracked file contains no matchable string and the test substitutes one at runtime.
Nothing credential-shaped is ever committed, and no exemption exists to erode.
"""

CLIENT = "@@KEY@@"
